### ripgrep — recursive content search.
#
# ripgrep has no default configuration path: it reads this file only because
# the Zsh module exports RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH in 30-env.zsh. Format is one
# command-line argument per line, `#` starts a comment, and the rest of the
# line is taken literally with no shell parsing — quotes become part of the
# value, so `--glob="!.git"` looks for a directory whose name really is
# `"!.git"`. Arguments here are prepended to the command line, so a flag
# passed to `rg` still wins over the same flag below.
#
# Every rg invocation reads this file, including the ones other programs make
# (fzf, Yazi, Neovim's pickers). Keep it to flags that decide which files are
# searched or how the pattern matches; never add output-shaping flags such as
# --heading, --pretty or --json, which break callers that parse rg's
# `file:line:column:text` output.

# Match case-insensitively while the pattern is entirely lowercase, and
# case-sensitively as soon as it contains an uppercase letter: `rg todo` also
# finds `TODO`, while `rg TODO` no longer matches `todo`.
--smart-case

# Search dotfiles and dot-directories, which is where most of this repository
# lives. Ignore files are still honoured, so `.gitignore`d paths stay out.
--hidden

# --hidden alone would also descend into .git, which holds packed objects, logs
# and every historical version of the file being searched. Later globs win, so
# `rg --glob='.git/**' <pattern>` still reaches it on demand.
--glob=!.git/

# Minified assets, lock files and single-line data dumps would otherwise print
# one enormous line per match. Replace the tail of such a line with a marker
# instead of dropping the match, so the hit stays visible and greppable.
--max-columns=200
--max-columns-preview

# Retry with PCRE2 when a pattern uses look-around or back-references that the
# default Rust engine cannot compile, instead of exiting with a regex error.
# `rg '(?<=foo)bar'` works without passing -P by hand.
--engine=auto

### Type definitions for this repository's own file names.

# The installer's package manifests are one-token-per-line lists that no
# built-in type covers; `rg -tpkgs <pattern>` searches both flavours at once.
# `module.conf` needs no entry — the built-in `config` type already matches
# *.cfg, *.conf, *.config and *.ini.
--type-add=pkgs:packages.brew
--type-add=pkgs:packages.apt
